  insert into public.course_items (course_id, kind, position, title, content) select cid, 'topic', 17, $j$5.1 Narrative Writing: Plot, Setting and Detail$j$, $j${"readings": [{"title": "Acts 9 (King James Version)", "url": "/portal/bible/Acts/9"}], "body": "A narrative tells a true or imagined story. A strong narrative has a narrator (the voice telling the story), a setting (where and when), characters, a plot with a problem or conflict, rising action, a climax (the turning point), and a resolution that shows how things change. Writers bring a story alive with sensory details, which appeal to sight, sound, smell, taste and touch, and with dialogue and precise verbs. They also show instead of tell: instead of writing \"I was nervous,\" a writer shows shaking hands and a dry mouth.\n\nScripture contains powerful personal narrative. In Acts 9:3 we read of Saul on the road: \"And as he journeyed, he came near Damascus: and suddenly there shined round about him a light from heaven.\" Notice the setting, the sudden turning point and the sensory detail. Read Acts 9 in the King James Version.\n\nCompare these two openings:\n\nTelling: I was afraid on the first day of school.\nShowing: The hallway smelled like floor wax and new notebooks. My hands were cold, and I held my schedule so tightly that the paper crinkled.\n\nYour task: (1) Label the setting, one sensory detail and the conflict in the \"showing\" opening. (2) Rewrite this sentence to show instead of tell: \"The storm was scary.\" (3) Outline a true story from your life using the stages of plot: problem, rising action, climax and resolution.\n\nFlorida standards: ELA.10.C.1.2, ELA.K12.EE.3.1.", "video": "", "presenter": "Dr. Alex L. Jenkins Sr.", "standards": "ELA.10.C.1.2, ELA.K12.EE.3.1", "script": "Listen, everyone has a story, and a story well told can change a life. Today we learn how to write a narrative that a reader can see, hear and feel.\n\nA narrative tells a story, true or imagined. Every good narrative has a narrator, which is the voice telling the story. It has a setting, which is the where and when. It has characters. And it has a plot. The plot begins with a problem or conflict. The action rises as the problem grows. The climax is the turning point, the moment of greatest tension. And the resolution shows how things change.\n\nNow watch this. The secret of great narrative writing is the rule, show, do not tell. If I write, I was nervous, I told you the feeling. If I write, my hands shook and my mouth went dry, I let you feel it. Use sensory details. Sight, sound, smell, taste and touch. Use precise verbs. Instead of walked, you can write crept, marched or stumbled. And use dialogue, the actual words people say, to bring characters to life.\n\nLook at the two openings in the lesson. The telling version says, I was afraid on the first day of school. It is true, but it is flat. The showing version says, the hallway smelled like floor wax and new notebooks. My hands were cold, and I held my schedule so tightly that the paper crinkled. The setting is the hallway on the first day. The sensory details are the smell and the cold hands. The conflict is the fear, and we never needed the word afraid.\n\nScripture is full of narrative. In Acts chapter nine, verse three, we read of Saul on the road to Damascus. And as he journeyed, he came near Damascus: and suddenly there shined round about him a light from heaven. See the setting, a road near a city. See the turning point, the word suddenly. And see the sensory detail, a light from heaven. In one verse the whole story turns.\n\nNow plan your own story. Choose a true moment from your life that taught you something. Write the problem. Write two moments of rising action. Write the climax. And write the resolution, including what you learned. A narrative is not only what happened. It is what it meant.\n\nYour task today. Label the setting, a sensory detail and the conflict in my showing opening. Rewrite the sentence, the storm was scary, so that it shows. Then outline your own story. Take the four question check when you finish.\n\nYour life has chapters worth telling. Tell them well. Excellence, Faith, Purpose. I will see you in the next lesson."}$j$::jsonb where not exists (select 1 from public.course_items where course_id = cid and kind = 'topic' and title = $j$5.1 Narrative Writing: Plot, Setting and Detail$j$);
  insert into public.course_items (course_id, kind, position, title, content) select cid, 'topic', 18, $j$5.2 Informative Writing: Organizing and Explaining$j$, $j${"readings": [{"title": "Genesis 1 (King James Version)", "url": "/portal/bible/Genesis/1"}], "body": "Informative (or expository) writing explains a subject clearly and accurately. It does not try to win an argument. It teaches. A strong informative essay has an introduction that names the topic and the controlling idea (a thesis), body paragraphs that each develop one main point with facts, definitions, examples or quotations, transitions that connect the ideas, precise vocabulary, and a conclusion that sums up what the reader learned.\n\nA paragraph can be organized like a sandwich: a topic sentence (the main point), supporting details, and a concluding sentence. Organizing patterns include cause and effect, compare and contrast, order of importance and chronological order (Lesson 3.1).\n\nScripture shows orderly explanation. Genesis 1:1 begins: \"In the beginning God created the heaven and the earth.\" Genesis 1:3 continues: \"And God said, Let there be light: and there was light.\" The chapter moves day by day in order. Read Genesis 1 in the King James Version.\n\nRead this original model paragraph:\n\nHoney bees are among the most organized insects on Earth. Each hive has three kinds of bees. The queen lays the eggs. Workers gather nectar, build the comb and care for the young. Drones exist mainly to mate with a queen. Because each kind has its own job, the hive survives through winter and summer alike.\n\nYour task: (1) Underline the topic sentence and the concluding idea. (2) Name the organizing pattern. (3) Write a topic sentence for an informative paragraph about a subject you know well, then add three supporting details.\n\nFlorida standards: ELA.10.C.1.4, ELA.K12.EE.3.1.", "video": "", "presenter": "Dr. Alex L. Jenkins Sr.", "standards": "ELA.10.C.1.4, ELA.K12.EE.3.1", "script": "Now, let us talk about writing that teaches. Informative writing explains a subject so well that the reader says, now I understand. It is not trying to win an argument. It is trying to light a lamp.\n\nA strong informative essay has an introduction that names the topic and gives a thesis, the controlling idea. It has body paragraphs, each with one main point, supported by facts, definitions, examples or quotations. It has transitions that connect the ideas. It uses precise vocabulary. And it ends with a conclusion that reminds the reader what was learned.\n\nHere is a trick for building a paragraph. Think of a sandwich. The top bread is the topic sentence, the main point. The filling is the supporting details. The bottom bread is the concluding sentence. Every sentence in the middle must belong to the main point. If a sentence does not belong, take it out.\n\nWatch this. You also choose an organizing pattern. If you are explaining how something happened, use chronological order. If you are showing why, use cause and effect. If you are showing differences, use compare and contrast. If the ideas are not equal, use order of importance. Remember Lesson three point one on text structures. The same patterns that help you read now help you write.\n\nLook at my model about honey bees. Honey bees are among the most organized insects on Earth. That is the topic sentence. Then I name three kinds of bees, the queen, the workers and the drones, and what each does. Those are the supporting details. The last sentence says, because each kind has its own job, the hive survives through winter and summer alike. That is the conclusion, and the words because each kind has its own job show cause and effect.\n\nScripture models order. Genesis chapter one, verse one says, In the beginning God created the heaven and the earth. Verse three says, And God said, Let there be light: and there was light. The chapter moves from day to day in order. It is a perfect example of chronological explanation.\n\nYour task today. Find the topic sentence and the concluding idea in my model, and name the pattern. Then write your own topic sentence on a subject you know well and add three supporting details. Take the four question check when you finish.\n\nClear explanation is a form of service. Serve your reader well. Excellence, Faith, Purpose. I will see you in the next lesson."}$j$::jsonb where not exists (select 1 from public.course_items where course_id = cid and kind = 'topic' and title = $j$5.2 Informative Writing: Organizing and Explaining$j$);
  insert into public.course_items (course_id, kind, position, title, content) select cid, 'topic', 19, $j$5.3 Planning, Revising and Editing$j$, $j${"readings": [{"title": "Psalm 19 (King James Version)", "url": "/portal/bible/Psalms/19"}], "body": "Good writers do not write once and stop. They move through a process: prewriting (brainstorming and outlining), drafting, revising (improving ideas, organization and word choice), editing (fixing grammar, spelling and punctuation), and publishing. Revising and editing are different jobs. Revising asks: Is my main idea clear? Do I have enough evidence? Is the order logical? Editing asks: Are my sentences correct?\n\nPsalm 19:14 gives a writer's prayer: \"Let the words of my mouth, and the meditation of my heart, be acceptable in thy sight, O LORD, my strength, and my redeemer.\" Read Psalm 19 in the King James Version.\n\nRevision checklist: Does the introduction state a clear claim or thesis? Does each paragraph have one main idea? Is there enough evidence? Are transitions used? Is the conclusion strong? Can I cut any sentence that does not help?\n\nEditing checklist: Does every sentence start with a capital letter and end with correct punctuation? Do subjects and verbs agree? Are words spelled correctly? Have I read it aloud?\n\nRead this rough sentence group and improve it: \"The bees is important. They make honey. Honey is good. Bees also helps flowers grow.\"\n\nYour task: (1) Revise the group into two smooth sentences that combine ideas. (2) Edit for subject and verb agreement. (3) Make a revision checklist of three questions you will ask about your next essay.\n\nFlorida standards: ELA.10.C.1.5, ELA.K12.EE.4.1.", "video": "", "presenter": "Dr. Alex L. Jenkins Sr.", "standards": "ELA.10.C.1.5, ELA.K12.EE.4.1", "script": "Listen, no great writer ever wrote a perfect first draft. The secret is the process. Today we learn to plan, revise and edit like a professional.\n\nThe writing process has five stages. Prewriting, where you brainstorm and outline. Drafting, where you get your ideas on the page without worrying about perfection. Revising, where you improve the ideas, the order and the word choice. Editing, where you fix the grammar, spelling and punctuation. And publishing, where you share your work.\n\nNow watch this. Revising and editing are not the same job. Revising is big picture. Is my main idea clear? Do I have enough evidence? Is the order logical? Is every sentence helping? Editing is small picture. Is each sentence correct? Do the subjects and verbs agree? Is the spelling right? Many students edit too early. They polish a sentence that should have been deleted. Revise first. Edit last.\n\nHere is a revision checklist. Does my introduction state a clear claim or thesis? Does each paragraph have one main idea? Do I have enough evidence? Do I use transitions? Is my conclusion strong? And is there a sentence I can cut? Cutting is one of the bravest acts of a writer.\n\nHere is an editing checklist. Capital letters at the start, correct punctuation at the end. Subjects and verbs that agree. Correct spelling. And read it out loud. Your ear will catch what your eye missed.\n\nNow let us practice. The rough group says, The bees is important. They make honey. Honey is good. Bees also helps flowers grow. Revise first. Combine the ideas. Bees are important because they make honey and help flowers grow. Now edit. Notice the verb is has become are, so it agrees with the plural subject bees. Two short and choppy sentences became one strong sentence.\n\nThe Psalmist gives writers a prayer. Psalm nineteen, verse fourteen. Let the words of my mouth, and the meditation of my heart, be acceptable in thy sight, O LORD, my strength, and my redeemer. Revise and edit as if your words will be read in the sight of God, because they will.\n\nYour task today. Revise and edit the rough group. Then write three revision questions for your next essay. Take the four question check when you finish.\n\nPatience with your draft is respect for your reader. Excellence, Faith, Purpose. I will see you in the next lesson."}$j$::jsonb where not exists (select 1 from public.course_items where course_id = cid and kind = 'topic' and title = $j$5.3 Planning, Revising and Editing$j$);
  insert into public.course_items (course_id, kind, position, title, content) select cid, 'topic', 20, $j$5.4 Conventions: Sentences, Punctuation and Style$j$, $j${"readings": [{"title": "James 3 (King James Version)", "url": "/portal/bible/James/3"}], "body": "Strong writing follows the conventions of standard English and uses varied, purposeful sentences. A simple sentence has one independent clause. A compound sentence joins two independent clauses with a comma and a coordinating conjunction (for, and, nor, but, or, yet, so) or with a semicolon. A complex sentence has an independent clause and a dependent clause that begins with a word such as because, although, when or if.\n\nCommon punctuation rules: Use a comma after an introductory word or phrase (\"However, the team won.\"). Use a comma before the conjunction in a compound sentence. Use a semicolon between two closely related independent clauses. Use a colon after a complete statement to introduce a list or explanation. Use apostrophes for possession (the student's book, the students' books), not for plurals.\n\nAvoid sentence errors: a fragment is an incomplete sentence; a run on joins independent clauses with no punctuation; a comma splice joins them with only a comma.\n\nJames 3:5 reminds us of the power of speech: \"Even so the tongue is a little member, and boasteth great things.\" Read James 3 in the King James Version. Careful words matter.\n\nYour task: (1) Fix this comma splice: \"The test was hard, I studied for it.\" (2) Combine these two short sentences into a complex sentence: \"I was tired. I finished my homework.\" (3) Write one compound sentence, one complex sentence and one sentence with a colon.\n\nFlorida standards: ELA.10.C.3.1, ELA.K12.EE.4.1.", "video": "", "presenter": "Dr. Alex L. Jenkins Sr.", "standards": "ELA.10.C.3.1, ELA.K12.EE.4.1", "script": "Now, let us talk about the tools of the craft. Conventions are the agreed rules of written English. When we follow them, the reader can focus on our ideas, not on our mistakes.\n\nFirst, sentence variety. A simple sentence has one independent clause. The team won. A compound sentence joins two independent clauses with a comma and a conjunction like and, but, or, so, or with a semicolon. The team won, and the crowd cheered. A complex sentence has an independent clause and a dependent clause, which begins with a word such as because, although, when or if. Because the team practiced, it won. Good writers mix all three, so the writing does not sound like a drumbeat.\n\nSecond, punctuation. Use a comma after an introductory word or phrase. However, the team won. Use a comma before the conjunction in a compound sentence. Use a semicolon between two closely related independent clauses. Use a colon after a complete statement to introduce a list or explanation. And remember that an apostrophe shows possession. It does not make a plural. The student's book means one student. The students' books means many students.\n\nWatch this. Three common errors. A fragment is an incomplete sentence, such as, because it rained. A run on joins independent clauses with no punctuation at all. And a comma splice joins two independent clauses with only a comma. Look at the one in the lesson. The test was hard, I studied for it. Two complete thoughts stuck together with a comma. Fix it three ways. Add a conjunction, the test was hard, so I studied for it. Use a semicolon, the test was hard; I studied for it. Or make two sentences.\n\nNow combine. I was tired. I finished my homework. Make it complex. Although I was tired, I finished my homework. The little word although shows the relationship between the ideas, and that is what good style does.\n\nJames chapter three, verse five says, Even so the tongue is a little member, and boasteth great things. Little things have great power. A comma can change a meaning. A word can change a day. Take the care that your words deserve.\n\nYour task today. Fix the comma splice. Combine the two short sentences. Then write one compound sentence, one complex sentence and one sentence with a colon. Take the four question check when you finish.\n\nCare in small things is the mark of excellence. Excellence, Faith, Purpose. I will see you in the next lesson."}$j$::jsonb where not exists (select 1 from public.course_items where course_id = cid and kind = 'topic' and title = $j$5.4 Conventions: Sentences, Punctuation and Style$j$);
  insert into public.course_items (course_id, kind, position, title, content) select cid, 'assessment', 5, $j$Assessment 5: Write a Personal Narrative$j$, $j${"prompt": "Write a personal narrative of 350 to 450 words about a true moment in your life that taught you something about faith, courage, responsibility or purpose. Write in first person.\n\nYour narrative must include: a clear setting; a problem or conflict; rising action; a climax; a resolution that shows what changed or what you learned; at least four sensory details; at least one line of dialogue; and showing instead of telling. Use a variety of sentence types. Revise your draft at least once, then edit for grammar, spelling and punctuation. Read it aloud before you submit.", "rubric": "100 points. Setting, conflict, rising action, climax and resolution: 25 points. Sensory detail and showing instead of telling: 20 points. Reflection on what was learned: 15 points. Dialogue and precise word choice: 10 points. Organization and flow: 10 points. Conventions (grammar, spelling, punctuation) and sentence variety: 20 points. A score of 70 or above is passing."}$j$::jsonb where not exists (select 1 from public.course_items where course_id = cid and kind = 'assessment' and title = $j$Assessment 5: Write a Personal Narrative$j$);
  insert into public.course_items (course_id, kind, position, title, content) select cid, 'discussion', 5, $j$Discussion 5: The Power of a Story Well Told$j$, $j${"prompt": "Think about a story, testimony or narrative that stayed with you, whether from Scripture, family, a book or your own life. Write a post of at least 120 words that (1) names the story and who told it, (2) tells what made it powerful, such as a vivid detail, a turning point or an honest ending, (3) names one writing skill from this unit that you will use in your own writing, and (4) explains why. Then write a respectful reply of at least 40 words to another student's post, or answer this question: Why do stories sometimes teach us more than lectures do? Keep your language clean and your tone kind."}$j$::jsonb where not exists (select 1 from public.course_items where course_id = cid and kind = 'discussion' and title = $j$Discussion 5: The Power of a Story Well Told$j$);
  insert into public.course_items (course_id, kind, position, title, content) select cid, 'quiz', 21, $j$Check 5.1: Narrative Writing: Plot, Setting and Detail$j$, $j${"questions": [{"q": "The climax of a plot is:", "options": ["The list of characters", "The first sentence", "The turning point of greatest tension", "The title"]}, {"q": "Which sentence shows instead of tells?", "options": ["I felt bad.", "My palms were damp and my heart hammered.", "I was nervous.", "It was a hard day."]}, {"q": "Sensory details appeal to:", "options": ["Sight, sound, smell, taste and touch", "Only dates", "Only sight", "Only facts"]}, {"q": "The resolution of a narrative:", "options": ["Is the setting", "Lists the sources", "Shows how the conflict ends or changes", "Introduces the problem"]}]}$j$::jsonb where not exists (select 1 from public.course_items where course_id = cid and kind = 'quiz' and title = $j$Check 5.1: Narrative Writing: Plot, Setting and Detail$j$);
  insert into public.quiz_keys (item_id, answers, explains) select id, '{2,1,0,2}'::int[], array[$j$The climax is the turning point.$j$,$j$Concrete details let the reader feel the emotion.$j$,$j$Sensory details use the five senses.$j$,$j$The resolution wraps up the conflict.$j$] from public.course_items where course_id = cid and kind = 'quiz' and title = $j$Check 5.1: Narrative Writing: Plot, Setting and Detail$j$ on conflict (item_id) do nothing;
  insert into public.course_items (course_id, kind, position, title, content) select cid, 'quiz', 22, $j$Check 5.2: Informative Writing: Organizing and Explaining$j$, $j${"questions": [{"q": "The purpose of informative writing is to:", "options": ["Tell a joke", "Hide the topic", "Win an argument", "Explain a subject clearly and accurately"]}, {"q": "A topic sentence:", "options": ["Ends the essay", "Is always a question", "States the main point of a paragraph", "Lists sources"]}, {"q": "A thesis statement is:", "options": ["A closing question", "A single quote", "The controlling idea of the whole essay", "A title page"]}, {"q": "Which pattern fits an explanation of why a thing happened?", "options": ["Alphabetical order", "Cause and effect", "Random order", "Rhyme"]}]}$j$::jsonb where not exists (select 1 from public.course_items where course_id = cid and kind = 'quiz' and title = $j$Check 5.2: Informative Writing: Organizing and Explaining$j$);
  insert into public.quiz_keys (item_id, answers, explains) select id, '{3,2,2,1}'::int[], array[$j$Informative writing teaches.$j$,$j$The topic sentence gives the paragraph's main point.$j$,$j$The thesis controls the essay.$j$,$j$Cause and effect shows why.$j$] from public.course_items where course_id = cid and kind = 'quiz' and title = $j$Check 5.2: Informative Writing: Organizing and Explaining$j$ on conflict (item_id) do nothing;
  insert into public.course_items (course_id, kind, position, title, content) select cid, 'quiz', 23, $j$Check 5.3: Planning, Revising and Editing$j$, $j${"questions": [{"q": "Revising mainly focuses on:", "options": ["Only the title", "Only the font", "Ideas, organization and word choice", "Only spelling"]}, {"q": "Editing mainly focuses on:", "options": ["Adding a new topic", "Grammar, spelling and punctuation", "Choosing the thesis", "Finding sources"]}, {"q": "Which sentence has correct subject and verb agreement?", "options": ["The bees be important.", "The bees is important.", "The bees am important.", "The bees are important."]}, {"q": "A helpful way to catch errors is to:", "options": ["Read the draft aloud", "Skip the draft", "Delete the thesis", "Change the topic"]}]}$j$::jsonb where not exists (select 1 from public.course_items where course_id = cid and kind = 'quiz' and title = $j$Check 5.3: Planning, Revising and Editing$j$);
  insert into public.quiz_keys (item_id, answers, explains) select id, '{2,1,3,0}'::int[], array[$j$Revising improves the big picture.$j$,$j$Editing fixes errors.$j$,$j$A plural subject takes the plural verb are.$j$,$j$Reading aloud helps you hear mistakes.$j$] from public.course_items where course_id = cid and kind = 'quiz' and title = $j$Check 5.3: Planning, Revising and Editing$j$ on conflict (item_id) do nothing;
  insert into public.course_items (course_id, kind, position, title, content) select cid, 'quiz', 24, $j$Check 5.4: Conventions: Sentences, Punctuation and Style$j$, $j${"questions": [{"q": "A comma splice is:", "options": ["An opening phrase", "Two independent clauses joined with only a comma", "A fragment", "A list with commas"]}, {"q": "Which is a complex sentence?", "options": ["Although I was tired, I finished my homework.", "I was tired.", "Tired and done.", "I was tired, and I finished my homework."]}, {"q": "Where does an apostrophe belong to show one student owns a book?", "options": ["student's book", "students's book", "students book", "studen't book"]}, {"q": "A fragment is:", "options": ["An incomplete sentence", "A perfect sentence", "A very long sentence", "A sentence with a list"]}]}$j$::jsonb where not exists (select 1 from public.course_items where course_id = cid and kind = 'quiz' and title = $j$Check 5.4: Conventions: Sentences, Punctuation and Style$j$);
  insert into public.quiz_keys (item_id, answers, explains) select id, '{1,0,0,0}'::int[], array[$j$A comma alone cannot join two complete sentences.$j$,$j$It has a dependent clause beginning with although.$j$,$j$The apostrophe shows possession: student's.$j$,$j$A fragment lacks a complete thought.$j$] from public.course_items where course_id = cid and kind = 'quiz' and title = $j$Check 5.4: Conventions: Sentences, Punctuation and Style$j$ on conflict (item_id) do nothing;
  insert into public.course_items (course_id, kind, position, title, content) select cid, 'quiz', 25, $j$Unit Quiz 5: Writing Workshop$j$, $j${"questions": [{"q": "A narrative with a clear problem, a turning point and an ending has a strong:", "options": ["Plot", "Footnote", "Table of contents", "Thesis only"]}, {"q": "'My hands shook and my mouth went dry' is an example of:", "options": ["A fragment", "A comma splice", "A thesis", "Showing instead of telling"]}, {"q": "An informative paragraph usually begins with:", "options": ["A comma splice", "A topic sentence", "A rhyme", "A dialogue line only"]}, {"q": "Revising focuses on:", "options": ["Ideas, evidence and organization", "Only capital letters", "Only the font", "Only spelling"]}, {"q": "Editing focuses on:", "options": ["Adding a thesis", "Choosing a title only", "Finding a new topic", "Grammar, spelling and punctuation"]}, {"q": "Which sentence is a complex sentence?", "options": ["We stayed inside.", "Because it rained, we stayed inside.", "It rained, so we stayed inside.", "Rain and wind."]}, {"q": "A run on sentence:", "options": ["Is a fragment", "Joins independent clauses with no punctuation", "Uses a colon properly", "Is always correct"]}, {"q": "Psalm 19:14 is a prayer that the speaker's words be:", "options": ["Hidden from others", "Repeated daily without change", "Acceptable in the sight of the LORD", "Loud and long"]}]}$j$::jsonb where not exists (select 1 from public.course_items where course_id = cid and kind = 'quiz' and title = $j$Unit Quiz 5: Writing Workshop$j$);
  insert into public.quiz_keys (item_id, answers, explains) select id, '{0,3,1,0,3,1,1,2}'::int[], array[$j$Plot includes conflict, climax and resolution.$j$,$j$It shows the feeling through details.$j$,$j$The topic sentence states the main point.$j$,$j$Revising improves the big picture.$j$,$j$Editing corrects errors.$j$,$j$It has a dependent clause beginning with because.$j$,$j$A run on lacks needed punctuation or a conjunction.$j$,$j$The Psalmist prays for acceptable words and thoughts.$j$] from public.course_items where course_id = cid and kind = 'quiz' and title = $j$Unit Quiz 5: Writing Workshop$j$ on conflict (item_id) do nothing;
